\subsection{Taxa and missing clades in the same data.frame}{

\strong{A data.frame can describe both types of taxa}, so a single call prepares the lists for \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{phylograftR::graft_missing_taxa()}}
and \code{\link[=graft_missing_clades]{phylograftR::graft_missing_clades()}}, on the same backbone and with the same clades written with the same tips.
The type of each taxon is given by:
\itemize{
\item The column \code{Type}, with \code{"taxa"} (target clade) or \code{"clades"} (sister clade): the clade is then read from \code{Target_clade} or \code{Sister_clade}, respectively.
\item Otherwise, without \code{Type}, by the clade column that is filled in its row, when the data.frame has both \code{Target_clade} and \code{Sister_clade} columns.
\strong{Each row must fill exactly one of them} (the function stops if both or none are filled).
}

The columns specific to a type are only read for the taxa of this type (e.g., \code{Excluded_subclades} for taxa with a target clade), and are ignored for the others, with a warning if they are filled.
\code{allowed_states} is read for both types. A clade written in the column of the other type than \code{Type} is ignored with a warning.

The function returns:
\itemize{
\item \strong{A single \code{missing_taxa_list}} if all the taxa have the same type, whether \code{Type} is provided or not.
\item \strong{A list of two \code{missing_taxa_list}}, named \verb{$taxa} and \verb{$clades}, if both types are present.
Each one is a standard \code{missing_taxa_list}, and the taxa of both lists are named differently (names must be unique across the table).
Use \verb{$taxa} in \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{phylograftR::graft_missing_taxa()}} and \verb{$clades} in \code{\link[=graft_missing_clades]{phylograftR::graft_missing_clades()}}.
}

Clade labels are also checked across both lists (the same clade has the same label as a target clade and as a sister clade),
so that they can be matched with the names of \code{total_richness_per_clades}.

\strong{Recommended use:} provide the whole list (\verb{$taxa} and \verb{$clades}) to \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa_and_clades]{phylograftR::graft_missing_taxa_and_clades()}},
which grafts the missing taxa first and then the missing clades, with a single set of inputs.
The two steps can also be chained by hand: graft the missing taxa with \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{phylograftR::graft_missing_taxa()}},
then use the grafted phylogeny as the backbone to graft the missing clades with \code{\link[=graft_missing_clades]{phylograftR::graft_missing_clades()}}.
The clades are defined by tips of the backbone, so they stay valid even when the grafted phylogeny is used for the second step.
}

\subsection{Node IDs}{

Clades (\code{Target_clade}, \code{Sister_clade}, and each subclade of \code{Excluded_subclades}) are typically defined by (pairs of) tips.
However, the function also accepts clades defined by the ID of a node of \code{phy} instead of tips, in the same table, and even in the same cell
(e.g., \code{"50; t5, t12; t7"} for three excluded subclades defined respectively by an internal node, a pair of tips, a terminal tip label).
Node IDs are integers: \code{1} to N, with N being the number of tips (\code{ape::Ntip(phy)}) correspond to terminal nodes, and thus define single-tip clades.
Following IDs are the internal nodes, as shown by \code{ape::nodelabels()}, and correspond to clades with multiple descending species.

A value made only of digits is a node ID, so \strong{tip labels must include at least one other character than digits} to be distinguished.
A clade is defined by tips or by one node ID, not both, and one node ID defines the clade alone.
Node IDs are converted into tips before any other check, as done by \code{\link[=convert_nodes_to_tips]{phylograftR::convert_nodes_to_tips()}},
and the conversions are printed if \code{verbose = TRUE}.

The warnings and errors that follow, and the grafting functions, therefore use the tips that are traced back to your node IDs in the messages.
If a clade is already defined by tips in the table, a node ID of the same clade is converted into the same tips.
Convert \code{total_richness_per_clades} (if you use it) at the same time as the list, with \code{\link[=convert_nodes_to_tips]{phylograftR::convert_nodes_to_tips()}},
so the tips of the clades match between both objects. Alternatively, build it from a table of clades with
\code{\link[=build_total_richness_per_clades]{phylograftR::build_total_richness_per_clades()}}, which matches the clades with those of the \code{missing_taxa_list}.
}

\subsection{Fine-tuning the clades}{

Defining clades with tips or node IDs gives full control on the constraints of each taxon. \strong{Any node of \code{phy} can be used} as a \code{Target_clade},
as a \code{Sister_clade}, or as an excluded subclade, simply by writing its node ID (e.g., \code{"34"}), or two tips whose MRCA is the node (e.g., \code{"t8, t7"}).
The node does not need to match a taxonomic group: it can be a clade without a name, a clade that groups several taxonomic units (e.g., two neighboring genera),
a part of a taxonomic group only, or a clade you identified from the phylogeny itself or from another source (e.g., a molecular study).
Clades can be nested at any depth, and each taxon has its own set of excluded subclades, with or without their stem.

This is the best route to fine-tune the grafting of a limited number of taxa, but the clades have to be written for each taxon.
When the backbone is large, and taxonomic levels (e.g., Family, Subfamily, Tribe, Genus) are defined for its tips and for the taxa to graft, \strong{taxonomic tables are the go-to option}:
clades are retrieved from their taxonomic names, which ease the attribution of clades across many taxa.
See \code{\link[=import_taxonomic_table]{phylograftR::import_taxonomic_table()}} and \code{\link[=build_missing_taxa_list_from_taxonomy]{phylograftR::build_missing_taxa_list_from_taxonomy()}}.

Both routes return the same \code{missing_taxa_list}, so they can be combined: build a list from taxonomic tables, and then adjust the clades of a few taxa with node IDs or tips.
}

\subsection{Labels of the clades}{

Labels (\code{Target_clade_name}, \code{Sister_clade_name}, and \code{Excluded_subclades_names}) are optional but, when used, they identify the clades
across all the rows of the table: \strong{the same clade must always have the same label, and different clades must have different labels.}
A clade is identified by the MRCA node of its tips in \code{phy}, so the same clade can be defined by different pairs of tips, as long as they share this MRCA node.
The function stops if a label is used for different clades, or if a clade is given different labels, and lists the labels and tips in conflict.
This catches typos in the tips or in the labels. Subclades without a label are not checked.
}

\subsection{Validity checks}{

The function \strong{stops with an informative message} if:
\itemize{
\item Required columns are missing, or one \code{Taxon_name} is missing, duplicated, or already in \code{phy}.
\item A clade has no tips, or tips are repeated, or are absent from \code{phy}.
\item The number of values in \code{exclude_subclades_stem}, or of labels in \code{Excluded_subclades_names}, does not match the number of excluded subclades.
\item Labels are inconsistent (see above).
\item A cell of \code{allowed_states} has an empty state (check the commas) or repeated states.
\item An excluded subclade is identical to, or contains, the target clade of its taxon (no branch would remain).
A warning is issued if it is outside of the target clade (the exclusion has no effect).
}

The messages give the names of the problematic taxa and the numbers of the rows in the table.
}
}
\examples{
# Load a table of missing taxa as a data.frame in R
file_path <- system.file("extdata", "toy_missing_taxa.csv", package = "phylograftR")
missing_taxa_table <- read.csv(file_path)

# Convert it into a 'missing_taxa_list', checking it against the backbone phylogeny
missing_taxa_list <- build_missing_taxa_list(data = missing_taxa_table, phy = toy_backbone)
# Items are named after the taxa, tips are split, and excluded subclades are named
str(missing_taxa_list, 2)

# A table can also be built in R and then converted to a valid 'missing_taxa_list' object
my_table <- data.frame(Taxon_name = c("M1", "M2"),
                       Target_clade = c("t26, t18", "t5"),
                       Excluded_subclades = c("t10, t19; t26, t4", NA),
                       Excluded_subclades_names = c("group_1; group_2", NA))
my_missing_taxa_list <- build_missing_taxa_list(data = my_table, phy = toy_backbone)
str(my_missing_taxa_list, 2)

# Clades can also be defined by node IDs (digits only), alone or mixed with tips.
# Node IDs are converted into tips, and the conversions are printed.
ape::plot.phylo(toy_backbone, cex = 0.7, no.margin = TRUE)
ape::nodelabels(cex = 0.6, frame = "none", col = "blue")
my_table_nodes <- data.frame(Taxon_name = c("M1", "M2"),
                             Target_clade = c("34", "t5, t12"),
                             Excluded_subclades = c("50", NA))
str(build_missing_taxa_list(data = my_table_nodes, phy = toy_backbone), 2)

# A table can describe both types of taxa: here, with a column 'Type'.
# Taxa to graft within a target clade and taxa to graft as missing clades are returned as two lists, for the two grafting functions.
my_table_both <- data.frame(Taxon_name = c("M1", "M2", "M3"),
                            Type = c("taxa", "taxa", "clades"),
                            Target_clade = c("t26, t18", "t5, t12", NA),
                            Sister_clade = c(NA, NA, "t10, t19"),
                            Excluded_subclades = c("t26, t4", NA, NA))
lists <- build_missing_taxa_list(data = my_table_both, phy = toy_backbone)
str(lists, 2)

# Without 'Type', each row fills the clade column of its type
my_table_both$Type <- NULL
identical(build_missing_taxa_list(data = my_table_both, phy = toy_backbone), lists)

}
